package concept

quantization_of_electric_charge: {
  content: #"""
    Electric charge is *quantized*: the net charge of an ordinary isolated
    object is an integer multiple of the elementary charge magnitude $e$:

    $ q = n e, quad n in ZZ $

    Here $n$ is an integer and $e = 1.602176634 × 10^(-19) "C"$. A proton has
    charge $+e$, while an electron has charge $-e$. In everyday charging,
    objects usually gain or lose electrons, so their net charge changes in
    steps of size $e$.

    The steps are so small compared with the charge involved in most
    macroscopic situations that charge is often treated as if it could vary
    continuously. This approximation is useful for describing charge spread
    over a wire, a surface, or a volume.
  """#
  related: ["concept/continuous-charge-distribution"]
}
